{"text": "From: Slavitt, Josh on Won, Oct 13, 1997 3:17 AM\nSubject: Stan Glantz Posting\n\u2018To: Woodward, Ellis; Daregan, Karen; MeDan\nJohn; Winegarden, Wayn\nJohn; Mohrzann, Christ:\nEllen; Culley, Liz; Spector, Janes; Han, Vi\nPoole, Jay: Ga\n\nfel, Janice; Mihes, Tracy; Dunham,\nCooper, David; Borelli, Thoaas; Turner, Dan; Butler,\n\nPontarelli, Jim; Parrish, Steve:\nta, John; Collanore, Thomas; Knox, George; Portnoy, sharon\n\nOctober 10, 1997, Internet Discussion List\n\nWhat follows are sone suggestions based on ay experience of watching how\npeople and organizations beat the tobacco industry and achieve policy and\nprogras objectives that have actually reduced tobacco consumption. These\nsuggestions are based on the current situation and are designed to be\npragmatic.\n\nNONGOVERIMENTAL ORGRUIZANTONS SHOULD LEAD NOT FOLLOW CLINTON\n\nExcept for the ALA, the primary tone of coments out of the major health\n\u2018groups has simply been to support the Clinton Administration. Walle it is\nperfectly fine to support the Adzinistration when they do the right thing,\nthe public health groups should be setting the agenda and the standards of\nperformance, not sixply supporting the President. while supporting the FDA,\nthe Clinton Adninistration also had agreed to the $50 billion tax break and\nnow appears more than happy to give the tobacco companies inmnity. It was\nBruce Lindsay, the White House Counsel, who twisted args to get the health\ngroups in the negotiations over the deal. Deal proponents were justified in\nfeeling nore than a little stabbed in the back when Clinton backed away\nfrom the deal as written.\n\nHere in California, AVA (and later ACS) took the lead setting the tobacco\ncontrol agenda by forcing the Wilson Adniniatration to inplenent\nProposition 99, the state tobacco control program, They accomplished this\nby combining high public standing with principled denands that Wilson\nactually engage the tobacco industry in the state anti-tobacco campaign\n\u2018This effort was not easy, but it was successful. This situation represented\nf real change in perspective and behavior on the part of AIA and ACS, and\nit was very successful. There is no reason why public health groups could\nnot win similar victories on the national lovel. If anything it should be\neasier with Clinton than Wilson, since Clinton is more syapathetic with\nanti-tobacco goals. In any event, Clinton should be following, not leading,\n\nWOL8EZLOLOZ",
